 1 big thing: Trump says he&#x27;s at Iran crossroads 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photos: Getty Images President Trump believes he's approaching a critical juncture in the Iran war over whether to restart massive attacks in an effort to end the conflict, he told Axios' Barak Ravid today. 

 Trump said: "I have a big decision coming up. Do I want to go in and annihilate them [the Iranian regime] or do I not? It's a big decision. Anything could happen with me." 🇺🇳 Trump's comments come ahead of a planned meeting with leaders from six Gulf countries — Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait and Oman — on the sidelines of next week's UN General Assembly in New York.

 The meeting could shape the next phase of the war. If Trump wants to resume major combat operations, he'll need buy-in from his regional allies. The president has said several times in recent days that the war will end soon. Some U.S. officials warn that the conflict has settled into an unsustainable, neither-war-nor-peace stalemate, and they believe that Trump could return to major combat operations after the midterms if a deal isn't reached by then. 🌍 Trump made clear in the interview that he wants to use the UN meeting to hear directly from regional allies about the war's next steps.

 He said: "I want to find out where they are and how they are doing. We have been very protective of them." Trump declined to say whether he'll decide on the path forward before or after the midterms. Keep reading . 

 
 
